WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:04.800
Life imitated art when Paul Hogan&nbsp;
fell for his "Crocodile Dundee"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:09.217
love interest. There was just one&nbsp;
roadblock: his wife of 30 years.

00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:12.240
Paul Hogan told the BBC that a trip to New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.240
inspired "Crocodile Dundee's"&nbsp;
fish-out-of-water tale, saying,

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:20.460
"I felt like an alien from another planet, some&nbsp;
of the 'Bushie' guys I know would feel even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:24.720
out of place. There's a myth that there is&nbsp;
a real Crocodile Dundee, but there isn't."

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.780
Still, many believe Rod Ansell's 1977 survival&nbsp;
story was the inspiration for the character Mick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:36.600
Dundee. Ansell became famous in Australia&nbsp;
after surviving for seven weeks on limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.700
supplies in the wilderness after his boat was&nbsp;
capsized in the Northern Territory. Indigenous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.100
Aborigines ultimately rescued Ansell, but when&nbsp;
people learned that he survived off the land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:52.680
and even resorted to drinking buffalo blood,&nbsp;
his unlikely survival story fascinated them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:57.180
Television host Michael Parkinson interviewed&nbsp;
Ansell, who told viewers he slept on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.260
floor at the luxury hotel in Sydney, just&nbsp;
like Mick Dundee later did in the movie.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:06.060
In 1980, Ansell published his story in the&nbsp;
book, "To Fight the Wild," but Hogan never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:10.500
admitted that Ansell was his inspiration.&nbsp;
After "Crocodile Dundee" became a hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.800
Ansell grew bitter because he never&nbsp;
earned royalties from the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:18.720
According to the Herald Sun, in 1999,&nbsp;
Ansel died after ambushing police in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:23.340
drug-fueled shootout that left one police&nbsp;
officer dead and other civilians injured.

00:01:23.481 --> 00:01:29.040
The financing group that funded "Crocodile Dundee"&nbsp;
was the brainchild of Hogan's business partner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.140
John Cornell, who envisioned an inventive model&nbsp;
to finance the independent comedy. They actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.460
chose to not partner with the Australian Film&nbsp;
Commission because they wanted full creative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:44.820
control over the project. Instead, a Queensland&nbsp;
bank underwrote $7.1 million of the film's budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.820 --> 00:01:49.260
while Cornell found additional&nbsp;
funding from 1400 small investors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:53.520
including Michael Hutchence, the lead&nbsp;
singer of the Australian band INXS.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.900
After they completed the film, Cornell flew to&nbsp;
the U.S. to look for a distributor. According&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.860
to The New York Times, Paramount snapped up&nbsp;
the U.S. distribution rights of "Crocodile&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:06.540
Dundee" when 20th Century Fox turned Cornell&nbsp;
down after watching only 20 minutes of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:10.800
movie. After the first and second "Crocodile&nbsp;
Dundee" movies were huge commercial hits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.060
Cornell told The New York Times that the&nbsp;
Fox executive had been extremely rude.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:20.312
"I sometimes get pleasure from thinking about what&nbsp;
the look is like on his face at a time like this."

00:02:20.534 --> 00:02:26.160
The first scene in "Crocodile Dundee" has&nbsp;
a big Easter egg referencing Paul Hogan's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.000
life before acting. It happens when we see&nbsp;
the Sydney Harbour Bridge in the background&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.600
while Sue Charlton talks to her boyfriend&nbsp;
on the phone at the beginning of the film.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.500
According to the Canberra Times, before&nbsp;
he got his break in showbiz on the talent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.580
show "New Faces," Hogan worked as a&nbsp;
rigger on the iconic bridge. He told&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.580 --> 00:02:47.100
IGN that he did the show on a dare and won&nbsp;
over the audience by roasting the judges.

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:53.160
"I went on 'New Faces' to take the mickey&nbsp;
out of it. I'm one of the few people that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.160 --> 00:02:56.880
ever went on a talent quest that had&nbsp;
no intention or desire to win it."

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:00.660
After appearing on "New Faces," Hogan was&nbsp;
interviewed at the Sydney Harbour Bridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.660
for the show "A Current Affair," which&nbsp;
brought him to the attention of future&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.660 --> 00:03:08.700
business and creative partner John Cornell.&nbsp;
Cornell and Hogan started JP Productions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.620
and "The Paul Hogan Show" made Hogan famous&nbsp;
— first in Australia, and later in the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:17.940
after the sketch series became syndicated&nbsp;
internationally. Hogan then became the face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:23.100
of Australian tourism with an advertisement&nbsp;
inviting Americans to visit "the wonders down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:28.182
under." This would later set the stage for&nbsp;
the success of "Crocodile Dundee" in America.

00:03:28.334 --> 00:03:33.060
"Crocodile Dundee" was shot on location in&nbsp;
Australia and New York City. Production for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:37.260
the "outback" scenes was filmed in the isolated&nbsp;
Kakadu National Park in the Northern Territory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:41.280
which had been home to a uranium mine.&nbsp;
Craig Bolles, the Australian location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.200
scout for "Crocodile Dundee," selected the&nbsp;
isolated region because, as he explained,

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:50.340
"It showcased a part of Australia that I&nbsp;
don't think had been seen ... and it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:54.300
inherent in Paul's nature. He seemed&nbsp;
to fit into the landscape perfectly."

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:59.400
Although Kakadu National Park has since become&nbsp;
a popular tourist destination, Bolles explained,

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:03.600
"Kakadu was a very different place in&nbsp;
the 1980s to what it is today. Only the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:08.100
main road was [paved] and there were no hotel&nbsp;
facilities at all as far as I can remember."

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:12.060
Because they were so far from&nbsp;
hotels, everyone on the cast and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:16.500
crew slept in huts left behind by the&nbsp;
Pancontinental Mining Company. Despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.500 --> 00:04:19.800
the dilapidated condition of the camp,&nbsp;
the cast and crew made the best of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:23.400
spending their nights around a campfire&nbsp;
and creating a summer camp atmosphere.

00:04:23.771 --> 00:04:28.800
After test screenings with American audiences,&nbsp;
Paramount trimmed seven minutes from the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:33.000
to make an international cut. Barry London,&nbsp;
president of distribution at Paramount,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:36.480
told the New York Times that they had to&nbsp;
quicken the pace to suit U.S. viewers.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:41.520
Most of the cut screen time came from the first&nbsp;
half of the film where Mick Dundee is showing Sue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:45.960
Charlton around the Australian Outback. During the&nbsp;
test screenings, American audiences also struggled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:50.280
to understand the Australian slang in the film,&nbsp;
so much of that was cut as well. Additionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:54.600
the f-word was dropped from the movie so&nbsp;
it could have a PG-13 rating in the U.S.

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:58.500
Paramount added quotation marks around&nbsp;
"Crocodile" on the posters and the title&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.500 --> 00:05:03.734
card so everyone would understand it was a man's&nbsp;
nickname and not a movie about a crocodile.

00:05:03.734 --> 00:05:09.060
Paul Hogan had planned on using a real crocodile&nbsp;
for close-ups during the scene where Sue was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.060 --> 00:05:13.980
attacked. This simply wasn't in the cards, so&nbsp;
production purchased a mechanical crocodile for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:19.080
$45,000. Although the crocodile that attacks&nbsp;
Sue while she's cooling off in the water was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:23.460
animatronic, there were real crocodiles&nbsp;
all over the set. They hired an armed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:28.080
guard and a crocodile wrangler to protect&nbsp;
the production from croc interference. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:32.940
Hogan explained to Go Erie while promoting&nbsp;
"The Very Excellent Mr. Dundee" in 2021,

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:38.760
"We had guys up trees with rifles just in&nbsp;
case one came along and ruined everything."

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:45.060
Hogan told IGN that the water buffalo was the&nbsp;
real stubborn diva on set. During the interview,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:49.440
he explained that the scene where he&nbsp;
mesmerizes the buffalo took all day, adding,

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:55.440
"Cause if the buffalo doesn't want to do anything,&nbsp;
it weighs 2000 pounds and you know, it doesn't."

00:05:55.440 --> 00:06:00.180
Paul Hogan met his first wife Noelene Edwards&nbsp;
when they were teenagers at the swimming pool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:05.100
where he was a lifeguard, and they married&nbsp;
young in 1958. Hogan told Australian Story,

00:06:05.100 --> 00:06:10.080
"I had three sons by the time I was&nbsp;
22, so I had to grow up very fast."

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.900
Although Paul Hogan was still&nbsp;
married to Edwards while making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:17.220
"Crocodile Dundee," he ultimately left&nbsp;
his first wife for his co-star Linda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:23.220
Kozlowski. Hogan and Edwards had a very&nbsp;
public and ugly second divorce in 1989,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:28.154
with Australian media outlets reportedly&nbsp;
depicting him as abandoning his family.

00:06:28.710 --> 00:06:30.745
"I've got five children to think about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.745 --> 00:06:33.600
and they were just as hurt as&nbsp;
I was, but in a different way."

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:38.340
Hogan married Kozlowski in 1990 in&nbsp;
Australia during a private ceremony&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:42.240
at a mansion he built as a wedding gift&nbsp;
to his bride. They had a son named Chase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:47.700
and settled in Los Angeles, but eventually&nbsp;
divorced in 2014. Kozlowski told New Idea,

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:51.060
"Honestly, we just naturally&nbsp;
grew apart. [...] The divorce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.935
was completely amicable [...] Everybody's happy."

00:06:53.935 --> 00:06:59.700
"Crocodile Dundee" hit Australian&nbsp;
cinemas on April 24, 1986. Hogan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:03.360
predicted it would be a commercial&nbsp;
success, telling Cinema Papers,

00:07:03.360 --> 00:07:06.720
"I expect it to gross millions&nbsp;
of dollars around the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:10.200
and I'm planning for it to be&nbsp;
Australia's first proper movie."

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:15.420
Hogan was right. Arriving in the U.S. in&nbsp;
September, "Crocodile Dundee" was one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:20.760
highest-grossing films of 1986 and was only&nbsp;
beaten at the U.S. box office by "Top Gun."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:25.800
It remains to this day the highest-grossing&nbsp;
Australian film at the Australian box office.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:28.560
The film's success came with a downside:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:33.360
unrealistic expectations. In&nbsp;
2022 Hogan told Mark Meets,

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:38.820
"Because of the ridiculous success of Dundee,&nbsp;
which is still the most successful independent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:43.500
film ever made, it's sort of like, 'Oh,&nbsp;
whatever I do next will be a flop by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:48.323
comparison. So why bother?' So mentally&nbsp;
I sort of retired after the first one."

00:07:48.323 --> 00:07:53.520
U.S. audiences were already familiar with Paul&nbsp;
Hogan before "Crocodile Dundee" came out because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:58.020
of the Australian tourism advertisements&nbsp;
he donated his time to during the '80s.

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:00.360
"You see, Australia is the world's biggest island,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:05.640
and as you can appreciate, the accents&nbsp;
varies noticeably from one place to another."

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:10.680
When "Crocodile Dundee" became an&nbsp;
international success, it altered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:15.480
tourism in Australia. In 2016, tourism&nbsp;
chief executive Tony Mayell told Perth Now,

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:18.060
"The film helped put Australia on the map."

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:22.380
According to The Guardian, they even started&nbsp;
giving "Crocodile Dundee" tours in Kakadu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:27.000
National Park, where the movie was filmed, and in&nbsp;
Darwin, where you can have close encounters with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:32.160
live crocodiles. Three hours away in Jabiru,&nbsp;
you can stay in the indigenous-owned Kakadu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:35.940
Crocodile Hotel, which is shaped like a&nbsp;
crocodile. From there, you can explore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.940 --> 00:08:41.040
the wilderness of Kakadu through guided tours,&nbsp;
scenic flights, or arts and crafts lessons with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:46.740
indigenous Aboriginal tribes who have lived in&nbsp;
the Northern Territory for at least 40,000 years.

00:08:46.935 --> 00:08:52.980
The film's sequel, "Crocodile Dundee II,"&nbsp;
was a top five box office hit in 1988,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:56.400
but after that, the franchise&nbsp;
seemed dead. Then in 2001,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:00.840
"Crocodile Dundee in Los Angeles" came out&nbsp;
and proved without a doubt that it was.

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:08.040
Nominated in 2002 for the Razzie for worst sequel&nbsp;
or remake and sitting at 11% on Rotten Tomatoes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:12.120
"Crocodile Dundee in Los Angeles"&nbsp;
was a box office disappointment and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.120 --> 00:09:16.320
critical disaster. The film's production was&nbsp;
troubled as well. According to Den of Geek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:20.940
there was a dispute over the script between&nbsp;
screenwriting duo Matthew Berry and Eric Abrams&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:25.680
and Hogan. Hogan extensively rewrote their&nbsp;
screenplay and believed he should receive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:30.180
the writing credit. Ultimately, the Writers Guild&nbsp;
sided with the original scriptwriters. They gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.180 --> 00:09:35.100
Berry and Abrams credit despite Hogan appealing&nbsp;
the decision. Hogan told The Los Angeles Times,

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:39.720
"Anyone who ever saw the first two movies will&nbsp;
know who wrote whatever comes out of anyone's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:44.972
mouth, but because I didn't change the plot and I&nbsp;
am the producer, I was not credited as a writer."

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:49.980
In January 2018, teasers began dropping&nbsp;
on the internet for what seemed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:52.620
a "Crocodile Dundee" reboot starring Danny McBride&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:56.820
as Mick Dundee's son. It was followed&nbsp;
by a full trailer and a Super Bowl ad.

00:09:56.993 --> 00:09:58.915
"When your dad did it, he was much…"

00:09:58.915 --> 00:10:00.655
"Okay, well, my dad told me about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.655 --> 00:10:03.480
he was just like, yeah, I just came&nbsp;
up and was duh duh duh duh this."

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:06.600
But it was soon revealed to&nbsp;
be a 27 million dollar ad&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:10.560
campaign to revitalize Australian&nbsp;
tourism. McBride told Insider,

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:13.920
"It wasn't like I was looking to do&nbsp;
a Super Bowl commercial, but I just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:17.820
thought the concept of this was just too funny&nbsp;
to see what people's reaction would be like."

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:23.460
Australia's Minister for Trade, Tourism, and&nbsp;
Investment, Steven Ciobo, told Tourism Australia,

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:26.760
"This is the single largest&nbsp;
investment Tourism Australia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.760 --> 00:10:31.080
has ever made in the US market and one&nbsp;
we believe will grow annual spend by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:35.400
American visitors to $6 billion&nbsp;
[Australian] by the year 2020."

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:41.052
Fans hoping for an actual revival had their&nbsp;
hopes dashed, though, when Hogan told Today,

00:10:41.052 --> 00:10:42.432
"It's not gonna happen."

00:10:42.534 --> 00:10:47.700
In 2020, Paul Hogan starred in "The Very&nbsp;
Excellent Mr. Dundee," a satire where he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.700 --> 00:10:51.840
played a fictionalized version of himself.&nbsp;
The movie depicts him getting into hot water&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.840 --> 00:10:56.520
on social media and meeting a bunch of other&nbsp;
old comedy stars. Hogan told Boomer Magazine,

00:10:56.520 --> 00:11:00.480
"It's not a deep meaningful movie,&nbsp;
just a bit of fun. While it pokes fun&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:04.500
at Hollywood rules and how social media&nbsp;
through the modern online community can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.500 --> 00:11:07.620
give you a reputation that you don't&nbsp;
deserve, it's mostly aimed at me."

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:12.300
Hogan making himself the butt of the joke is&nbsp;
certainly familiar ground for the Aussie whose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:16.380
self-deprecating humor won audiences back&nbsp;
in the '70s and '80s, though critics panned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:20.700
"The Very Excellent Mr. Dundee," giving&nbsp;
it abysmal scores on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:23.340
In 2020 Hogan also released his memoir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:27.960
"The Tap-Dancing Knife Thrower: My Life&nbsp;
(without the boring bits)." Hogan told Go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.960 --> 00:11:31.980
Erie that it was the brainchild of his&nbsp;
longtime writing partner Dean Murphy.

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:36.600
"It was Dean again who talked me into doing&nbsp;
[the book], telling me that if I dictated it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:39.420
he'd write it down, so that&nbsp;
appealed to my lazy side."

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:42.840
Hogan might be done with Hollywood,&nbsp;
but that doesn't mean audiences have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.840 --> 00:11:46.200
forgotten him or that iconic&nbsp;
role from the land down under.

